      importance: 4,
      summary: "Tataka's son, twice beaten by Rama and terrified of him, who became the golden deer that drew Rama away from Sita.",
      bio: "Maricha is the son of the yakshi Tataka by Sunda, and he enters the epic in Bala Kanda. With his brother Subahu he pollutes the sacrifices of Vishwamitra at the Siddhashrama by raining flesh and blood on the altar. Vishwamitra brings the boy Rama to guard the rite, and on the sixth day the two rakshasas come. Rama kills Subahu with the Agneyastra but, deliberately choosing not to kill Maricha, strikes him with the Manavastra — the 'human' weapon — which hurls him a hundred yojanas out to sea. Valmiki makes this mercy the seed of everything later: Maricha survives, and he never forgets what hit him.\n\nYears afterwards he is living as an ascetic in the forest when Ravana comes to him with the plan to abduct Sita. Maricha's response, in Aranya Kanda sargas 36 to 38, is the most sustained argument against the abduction in the whole epic, and the most prophetic. He tells Ravana that Rama is dharma embodied, the king of all the world as Indra is of the gods; that he himself has twice felt Rama's arrows and still sees Rama in every tree and in his dreams; that he has fled to the forest in terror of the syllable 'ra'. He warns that Ravana will destroy Lanka, himself and his whole line, and he advises him plainly to go home.\n\nRavana refuses, and threatens to kill him if he does not comply. Maricha then makes the calculation Valmiki records in his own voice: better to die at Rama's hand than at Ravana's, since death from a noble enemy at least ends well. He takes the form of a deer of unearthly beauty — gold-skinned, silver-spotted, antlers like sapphire — and grazes near the Panchavati hut until Sita sees it and asks Rama to catch it. Rama pursues it a long way, recognises the trick, and brings it down with an arrow. Dying, Maricha performs his last service: he resumes his rakshasa form and cries out in a perfect imitation of Rama's voice, 'Ah Sita! Ah Lakshmana!' Sita hears it, and the quarrel that drives Lakshmana from her side follows directly.",
